<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#norman-architecture">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Norman architecture</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Norman Romanesque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Norman architecture is the Romanesque style of building developed in Normandy in the eleventh century and carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after 1066 and to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. It is marked by massive walls, round arches, cylindrical piers and early rib vaults, as seen at the abbeys of Jumièges and Saint-Étienne in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> and in English cathedrals such as Durham.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/norman-architecture.jpg" alt="Norman architecture" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver-Bonjoch · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durham_Cathedral._Interior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> arrived at the mouth of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in 911, and over the following century the people of the territory ceded to them, now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>, adopted Frankish customs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and the langue d'oïl. Norman barons built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> castles on earthen mounds, beginning the development of the motte-and-bailey castle, and great stone churches in the Romanesque style of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>; by 950 they were building stone keeps. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caen">Caen</a> was the centre of the style, with its castle and two wealthy ducal abbeys, the Abbaye aux Dames and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-etienne-caen">Abbaye aux Hommes</a>, whose large churches with three-storey elevations and towered façades became models for the English cathedrals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The buildings show massive proportions in simple geometries with small bands of sculpture. Arches are semicircular, early ones plain and square-edged, later ones enriched with zig-zag and roll mouldings. Massive piers are either clusters of shafts or cylinders, sometimes incised, notably at Durham Cathedral. Windows are small and narrow, usually a single round-headed light. Main doorways have a succession of receding arches, often with chevron mouldings, and sometimes a tympanum carved with a Biblical scene. Churches were cruciform, often with a deep chancel and a square crossing tower. Though now mostly bare stone, the buildings would have been plastered and painted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>, brought up in Normandy, brought masons in 1042 to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, the first Romanesque building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After the Conquest of 1066 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> replaced every English bishop but one, rebuilt every cathedral, starting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> after a fire in 1067, and almost every monastic church, and built hundreds of parish churches and a chain of castles. Durham Cathedral, begun in 1093, was the first to use a ribbed vault with pointed arches. The style spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> under David I and, after 1169, to Ireland with Anglo-Norman settlers.</p><p>After a fire at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> in 1174 William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sens">Sens</a> introduced Gothic architecture, and Romanesque had virtually disappeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> by the early 13th century, after decades of overlap known as the Transitional Style.</p><p>The word Norman for the architecture may come from eighteenth-century antiquarians, and Thomas Rickman gave it a place in a sequence of styles in 1817. Earlier writers often mistook Norman buildings for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a>. In the nineteenth century a Neo-Norman revival used the style for buildings needing heavy massiveness, such as Lewes Prison, and also for Penrhyn Castle and the Natural History Museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Norman architecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Norman Romanesque</b> architecture, seen at Jumièges, at Saint-Étienne in Caen and in the cathedrals of England, shaped building across the Anglo-Norman world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
